From: xnulinux
Subject: [PATCH] linux64: a workloop sync waiter is not woken by being named as an inner queue's owner

macOS 27.2's libdispatch tells the kernel who owns a queue that was taken without a system call also when that queue
is not the workloop but one that targets it: a NOTE_WL_SYNC_WAKE | NOTE_WL_DISCOVER_OWNER event with bit 0x200, the
owner's thread as ident, that queue's state word as the address, sent together with the caller's own
NOTE_WL_SYNC_WAIT. This kernel does not know the bit. The owner of an inner queue, unlike the owner of the workloop,
can be waiting on the workloop at that moment (dispatch_sync on the inner queue, then stuck behind the workloop's
owner: its on-stack record is linked on the workloop's list), and its waiting knote has the same ident. The event was
taken as a wake-up of that knote: the owner returned from its wait as if the workloop had been handed to it, ran its
block and returned, and its record stayed on the list. Whoever drained the workloop later took the record off it, by
then stack that had been reused, and died authenticating its first word (EXC_ARM_PAC_FAIL at
_dispatch_lane_serial_drain + 212: locationd in about half of the boots with four CPUs, never with one).

filt_wltouch now leaves a knote that is already there alone when such an event arrives, and a knote that such an
event made is not a wake-up for its owner's later wait unless a real NOTE_WL_SYNC_WAKE came in between.
---
--- a/bsd/kern/kern_event.c
+++ b/bsd/kern/kern_event.c
@@ -2050,6 +2050,20 @@ filt_wlupdate_inheritor(struct kqworkloop *kqwl, struct turnstile *ts,
 	turnstile_update_inheritor(ts, inheritor, flags);
 }
 
+/*
+ * LINUX64: macOS 27.2's libdispatch sets this bit (no public header names it; the name is ours) on the
+ * NOTE_WL_SYNC_WAKE | NOTE_WL_DISCOVER_OWNER event that tells the kernel who owns a queue taken without a system
+ * call, when that queue is not the workloop itself but one that targets it (EV_EXTIDX_WL_ADDR is that queue's
+ * state word; the event comes with the caller's own NOTE_WL_SYNC_WAIT). The event's ident is the owner's, as a
+ * waiter's is, and unlike the owner of the workloop the owner of an inner queue can be waiting on the workloop at
+ * that moment (dispatch_sync on the inner queue, then stuck on the workloop: its record is linked on the workloop's
+ * list). Taken as a plain NOTE_WL_SYNC_WAKE, the event found the owner's waiting knote and woke it: the owner went on
+ * as if it had been handed the workloop, and whoever drained the workloop later found its record, by then stack that
+ * had been reused (EXC_ARM_PAC_FAIL in _dispatch_lane_serial_drain, locationd in about half of the boots with four
+ * CPUs).
+ */
+#define NOTE_WL_INNER_OWNER 0x00000200
+
 #define EVFILT_WORKLOOP_EFAULT_RETRY_COUNT 100
 #define FILT_WLATTACH 0
 #define FILT_WLTOUCH  1
@@ -2720,6 +2734,20 @@ filt_wltouch(struct knote *kn, struct kevent_qos_s *kev)
 	}
 
 	uint32_t command = kev->fflags & NOTE_WL_COMMANDS_MASK;
+	/*
+	 * LINUX64: an owner told about with NOTE_WL_INNER_OWNER may itself be waiting on this workloop, or come to
+	 * (see NOTE_WL_INNER_OWNER), and this knote is the owner's. Being discovered changes nothing for a knote
+	 * that is already there: a waiting owner is not woken by it. A knote the discovery made (kn_sfflags keeps the
+	 * bit) is not a wake-up either: the owner's wait on it waits, unless a real NOTE_WL_SYNC_WAKE came in between.
+	 */
+	if (command == NOTE_WL_SYNC_WAKE) {
+		if (kev->fflags & NOTE_WL_INNER_OWNER) {
+			return 0;
+		}
+		kn->kn_sfflags &= ~NOTE_WL_INNER_OWNER;
+	} else if (command == NOTE_WL_SYNC_WAIT && (kn->kn_sfflags & NOTE_WL_INNER_OWNER)) {
+		kn->kn_sfflags &= ~(NOTE_WL_INNER_OWNER | NOTE_WL_SYNC_WAKE);
+	}
 	if (command == NOTE_WL_SYNC_IPC) {
 		error = filt_wlupdate_sync_ipc(kqwl, kn, kev, FILT_WLTOUCH);
 	} else {
